    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A common strategy for many recent migrants is the clustering around a particular locale\r\nwith other co-ethnic members as a way to make living in their new environment much more\r\npalatable. This includes the formation of certain ethnic enclaves, common in many urban centers\r\nthat include Chinatowns, Little Mexicos, or Little Italys, that serve as a necessary cultural hub\r\nthat provides information on resources like housing, job opportunities, or support networks for\r\nother arriving co-ethnic migrants. In some cases, migrants even cluster around a particular\r\nprofession that often become synonymous with said groups. In my thesis, I focus on Mexican\r\nIce-Cream street vendors, colloquially known as Paleteros, in order to better understand the\r\nhistory of recent Mexican immigration and the social conditions by which these migrants choose\r\nstreet food vending, an otherwise highly visible profession. I play close attention to the\r\ninception, growth, and proliferation of the Ice-Cream street vending business by interviewing\r\nboth street vendors and ice-cream street vending business owners located throughout the San\r\nFrancisco Bay Area. In doing so, the ensuing thesis strives to add to the growing literature on\r\nstudies pertaining to small, informal businesses that are largely operated by Latinx immigrants as\r\nwell as examining the historical and social conditions that promulgated an otherwise\r\nunderstudied topic",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Mora, A. (2023). Paleteros: A History of Mexican Ic eCream Street Vendors in the San Francisco Bay Area. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/fn592fx1672. https://doi.org/10.25740/fn592fx1672.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
